/span></span></h2>
<p style=3D"mso-line-height-rule:exactly; font-size:16px; line-height:175%"=
><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">In a landmark move,=
 The New York Times Company has entered a multiyear agreement with Amazon t=
o license its editorial content for use across Amazon=E2=80=99s AI platform=
s. The deal includes not just news articles, but also content from <em>NYT =
Cooking</em> and <em>The Athletic</em>.</span></p>
<p style=3D"mso-line-height-rule:exactly; font-size:16px; line-height:175%"=
><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">This marks the firs=
t time The Times has licensed its content specifically for generative AI us=
e.</span></p>
<p style=3D"mso-line-height-rule:exactly; font-size:16px; line-height:175%"=
><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">In a twist of irony=
, The Times sued OpenAI and Microsoft in 2023 for allegedly using its conte=
nt to train AI models without permission. Fast-forward to now, and it's for=
ging a paid arrangement with another tech giant.</span></p>
<p style=3D"mso-line-height-rule:exactly; font-size:16px; line-height:175%"=
><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">Financial terms wer=
e not disclosed. But according to The Times, the agreement ensures that exc=
erpts used on Amazon platforms like Alexa will include attribution and link=
s back to the original content. Amazon may also use the material to train i=
ts proprietary AI models.</span></p>
<p style=3D"mso-line-height-rule:exactly; font-size:16px; line-height:175%"=
><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">CEO Meredith Kopit =
Levien stated, =E2=80=9CThe deal is consistent with our long-held principle=
 that high-quality journalism is worth paying for.=E2=80=9D</span></p>
<p style=3D"mso-line-height-rule:exactly; font-size:16px; line-height:175%"=
><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, sans-serif;">Amazon has not prov=
ided further comment beyond what was shared by The Times.</span></p>
